    `Father, I come to You today with my tongue — the small part of my body that James says can set a forest on fire. I bring it honestly, because I know that I have used it in ways that were not building up. The careless comment that seemed harmless. The piece of information shared that was not mine to share. The exaggeration in the retelling that made the story more interesting at someone else's expense. The argument I pursued past the point where resolution was the goal. I receive Ephesians 4:30 today as a sober reminder: the Holy Spirit can be grieved. Not by the sins I am most dramatic about, but by the words I treat as the most ordinary. Let that reality change how I speak today. Not through performance or self-monitoring but through the genuine desire to build rather than tear down, to unite rather than divide, to be someone in whose mouth the name and reputation of another person is safe. Set a guard over my mouth today, Lord. Not a lock — a guard. The guard of love that considers the impact of words before they leave. The guard of the Spirit that catches what my instinct would have let through.`
