    `Father, I come to You today with the anger, the pride, and the need to be right that I carry more than I would like to admit. I confess that my instinct when I am disagreed with is not always the humble mind of Christ. Sometimes it is the eritheia — the self-positioning, the need to win, the inability to let the other person be right without experiencing it as a personal diminishment. Give me the mindset of Christ Jesus. Not as a performance of humility that is secretly still proud, but as the genuine, Spirit-produced reorientation of a self that is learning to find its security in You rather than in its reputation, its correctness, or its standing in the eyes of others. The person who is genuinely secure in You has nothing to prove and nothing to protect. Let me be that person. Teach me to be quick to listen, slow to speak, and slow to become angry. Not because anger is always wrong but because human anger almost never produces the righteousness of God. Let me be slow enough to hear, humble enough to consider that I might be wrong, and secure enough in You that being wrong does not threaten anything that actually matters.`
